WEBVTT

NOTE Made-up call for the check-call-summary check set. Ostlund Haulage, Wheelrota and everyone named are fictional.

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:26.583
<v Ben Achebe>Hi both, thanks for making the time this morning.</v>

2
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:51.167
<v Priya Raman>No problem. Tom's just joining now.</v>

3
00:00:52.167 --> 00:01:15.750
<v Tom Hale>Morning. Sorry, I'm back to back today.</v>

4
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:40.333
<v Ben Achebe>Not at all. We said half an hour but I'll try to keep it to twenty. Priya, you filled in the form for Ostlund Haulage on our site, so maybe start with what prompted that?</v>

5
00:01:41.333 --> 00:02:04.917
<v Priya Raman>Sure. So we run a hundred and forty vehicles across nine depots, mostly rigids and a few artics.</v>

6
00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:29.500
<v Priya Raman>And maintenance is all tracked in spreadsheets. Each depot has its own, and they don't talk to each other.</v>

7
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:54.083
<v Ben Achebe>Got it. And where does it hurt most?</v>

8
00:02:55.083 --> 00:03:18.667
<v Priya Raman>Unplanned breakdowns, mainly. When a truck goes off the road mid-route we're paying for recovery, a hire vehicle, and usually a missed delivery window.</v>

9
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:43.250
<v Tom Hale>And tyres. Our tyre bill went up a lot last year and nobody can tell me why.</v>

10
00:03:44.250 --> 00:04:07.833
<v Ben Achebe>That's a common one. Tyre wear is usually the first thing that shows up once inspections are logged in one place.</v>

11
00:04:08.833 --> 00:04:32.417
<v Tom Hale>If you can fix our tyre bill I'll sign whatever you send me. In blood, if needed.</v>

12
00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:57.000
<v Priya Raman>[laughs] He won't.</v>

13
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:21.583
<v Tom Hale>I won't. But it is the number the board keeps asking me about.</v>

14
00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:46.167
<v Ben Achebe>Understood. Can I ask how inspections get recorded today, the daily walkaround checks?</v>

15
00:05:47.167 --> 00:06:10.750
<v Priya Raman>Paper, then someone types them up at the depot. Sometimes days later.</v>

16
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:35.333
<v Priya Raman>Honestly my biggest worry with any system is the drivers. They hate apps. We tried one a couple of years ago and nobody used it after the first month.</v>

17
00:06:36.333 --> 00:06:59.917
<v Ben Achebe>That's fair, and it's the thing that sinks most of these projects. Our driver check is three taps, it works offline, and we'd do a briefing at each depot.</v>

18
00:07:00.917 --> 00:07:24.500
<v Priya Raman>Three taps I'd want to see.</v>

19
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:49.083
<v Ben Achebe>You will. Who else is involved in a decision like this?</v>

20
00:07:50.083 --> 00:08:13.667
<v Ben Achebe>That's me up to twenty-five thousand pounds a year. Above that it goes to the board.</v>

21
00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:38.250
<v Priya Raman>And I'd be the one living with it day to day, so I'd want a say.</v>

22
00:08:39.250 --> 00:09:02.833
<v Ben Achebe>Of course. And timing, is there anything driving it?</v>

23
00:09:03.833 --> 00:09:27.417
<v Priya Raman>Our maintenance contract with the garage group renews on the thirty-first of March.</v>

24
00:09:28.417 --> 00:09:52.000
<v Priya Raman>We'd want to decide before the end of February so we're not renegotiating blind.</v>

25
00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:16.583
<v Ben Achebe>That's helpful. Are you looking at anyone else?</v>

26
00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:41.167
<v Tom Hale>We've got a demo booked with Wheelrota for next week. Priya set that up.</v>

27
00:10:42.167 --> 00:11:05.750
<v Priya Raman>Yes, Wednesday. Mainly to compare on price.</v>

28
00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:30.333
<v Ben Achebe>Makes sense. I won't knock them. I'd just ask you to look at how the driver side works in both.</v>

29
00:11:31.333 --> 00:11:54.917
<v Ben Achebe>What we usually suggest is a thirty-day pilot at two depots, so you get real data on breakdowns and tyres before you commit to anything.</v>

30
00:11:55.917 --> 00:12:19.500
<v Tom Hale>A pilot could work. I'd want to see a proposal first, and Priya needs to be comfortable with it.</v>

31
00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:44.083
<v Priya Raman>I'd be fine with a pilot if the drivers actually use it. Which depots would make sense?</v>

32
00:12:45.083 --> 00:13:08.667
<v Ben Achebe>Usually one of your busiest and one that's struggling. You'd know better than me.</v>

33
00:13:09.667 --> 00:13:33.250
<v Priya Raman>Probably Doncaster and Wakefield then.</v>

34
00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:57.833
<v Ben Achebe>On pricing, we charge per vehicle per month, and the pilot itself is free. If you go ahead after the pilot, the first year is priced on the full fleet.</v>

35
00:13:58.833 --> 00:14:22.417
<v Ben Achebe>Thanks, Ben, that's really clear.</v>

36
00:14:23.417 --> 00:14:47.000
<v Tom Hale>What does per vehicle look like for a fleet our size?</v>

37
00:14:48.000 --> 00:15:11.583
<v Ben Achebe>I'd rather put that in the proposal properly than guess on a call. It depends on the modules.</v>

38
00:15:12.583 --> 00:15:36.167
<v Tom Hale>Fine.</v>

39
00:15:37.167 --> 00:16:00.750
<v Priya Raman>Before you send anything, it might help if you see the data. I'll pull the last six months of maintenance logs together and send them over next week.</v>

40
00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:25.333
<v Ben Achebe>That would be brilliant. I'll get the pilot proposal over to you both by Thursday.</v>

41
00:16:26.333 --> 00:16:49.917
<v Priya Raman>Can we get something in the diary for the week after to go through it?</v>

42
00:16:50.917 --> 00:17:14.500
<v Ben Achebe>I'll send an invite for Tuesday the seventeenth, ten o'clock?</v>

43
00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:39.083
<v Priya Raman>That works.</v>

44
00:17:40.083 --> 00:18:03.667
<v Tom Hale>Works for me if it's before eleven.</v>

45
00:18:04.667 --> 00:18:28.250
<v Ben Achebe>Great. Anything else on your side?</v>

46
00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:52.833
<v Tom Hale>Only that whatever we do has to work with the depot managers, not around them.</v>

47
00:18:53.833 --> 00:19:17.417
<v Ben Achebe>Agreed. Thanks both, speak soon.</v>

48
00:19:18.417 --> 00:19:42.000
<v Priya Raman>Thanks, Ben. Bye.</v>
